1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,790
Somebody once told you that your morning
coffee is why you're broke.

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:05,570
$5 a day.

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:13,290
Invested instead, at 7% a year, for 30
years, that's about $172,000.

4
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:15,550
And here's the strange part.

5
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:19,040
That math is correct, but it's also one of

6
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:22,050
the most misleading ideas in personal
finance,

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:26,600
and believing it may be costing you far more
than the coffee ever could.

8
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:29,320
In the next few minutes, I'll show you the
numbers

9
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:32,090
that actually decide whether you build
wealth.

10
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,840
They're much bigger than a latte, and one
of them is probably

11
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:40,110
sitting on your phone right now, and most
people never look at it.

12
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:42,620
But first, let's talk about why your brain

13
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:45,070
fell for the coffee story in the first
place.

14
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:46,510
Meet your brain.

15
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:49,830
It loves the coffee story, for three
reasons.

16
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:51,970
One, you can see it.

17
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:54,780
You buy coffee every day, with your own
hands.

18
00:00:54,800 --> 00:01:00,030
A car payment leaves your account automatically
once a month, and you barely notice.

19
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:03,150
Two, skipping it feels like progress.

20
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:07,850
You say no to a latte, and you get a small
hit of, I'm being responsible.

21
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,770
Your brain counts that as a win, even if
nothing else changes.

22
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:13,890
Three, it's easy.

23
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:16,550
Cutting coffee asks nothing hard of you.

24
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:20,250
No awkward talks, no moving house, no
selling the car.

25
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:22,480
Your brain always picks the easy fight.

26
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,770
Psychologists call part of this mental
accounting.

27
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,390
We sort money into separate mental buckets,
and judge each one on its own.

28
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:32,230
The coffee bucket looks guilty.

29
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:36,370
Meanwhile, the bucket that's actually
draining you never gets checked.

30
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:37,970
So, let's check it.

31
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:40,690
According to the U.S. Bureau of Labor
Statistics,

32
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:45,070
the average American household spends about a
third of its budget on housing.

33
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,170
Transportation takes about one dollar in
six.

34
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:49,330
Food is next.

35
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:50,450
Daily coffee?

36
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:51,960
It doesn't even make the chart.

37
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:54,850
Here's the key idea, so remember it.

38
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:58,020
Cutting your biggest expense by 10%
usually

39
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:01,030
beats cutting your smallest expense by
100%.

40
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:05,080
Save 10% on rent, and you could free up
more money than giving

41
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:08,610
up coffee completely, without changing a
single morning.

42
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:11,730
But housing isn't the one I'm most worried
about for you.

43
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:13,030
This next one is.

44
00:02:13,668 --> 00:02:19,918
Experian reports that the average new car payment
in America is over $700 a month.

45
00:02:20,368 --> 00:02:24,778
Now imagine a reliable used car with a
payment closer to $400.

46
00:02:25,108 --> 00:02:28,758
That's about $340 a month back in your
pocket.

47
00:02:29,188 --> 00:02:31,618
That's over $4,000 a year.

48
00:02:32,048 --> 00:02:34,078
More than two years of daily coffee.

49
00:02:34,368 --> 00:02:36,138
Every single year.

50
00:02:36,648 --> 00:02:43,358
Invest that difference at the same 7% for 30
years and you get roughly $380,000.

51
00:02:43,808 --> 00:02:48,578
That's more than double the coffee fortune
and you never gave up a latte.

52
00:02:49,008 --> 00:02:50,798
So why do we do it?

53
00:02:51,128 --> 00:02:55,498
Because your brain prices the car by the
monthly payment, not the total.

54
00:02:55,888 --> 00:03:00,158
It's only $700 a month feels a lot like
it's only five bucks.

55
00:03:00,288 --> 00:03:02,858
It's the same trick, just bigger.

56
00:03:03,348 --> 00:03:06,648
And if the car feels expensive, the next
one is

57
00:03:06,668 --> 00:03:10,438
worse because you pay for it and get
nothing back.

58
00:03:10,848 --> 00:03:13,308
Picture paying for coffee every morning
for

59
00:03:13,328 --> 00:03:16,658
nine months and never getting a single
cup.

60
00:03:17,168 --> 00:03:19,758
That's roughly what credit card interest
looks like.

61
00:03:20,108 --> 00:03:24,568
Average credit card interest rates in the
U.S. are now above 20%.

62
00:03:24,588 --> 00:03:29,028
Carry a $6,000 balance at around 22% and
you're

63
00:03:29,048 --> 00:03:33,358
paying about $1,300 a year in interest
alone.

64
00:03:33,968 --> 00:03:36,858
That's around 264 coffees.

65
00:03:37,168 --> 00:03:38,658
Roughly nine months of them.

66
00:03:38,888 --> 00:03:39,378
Gone.

67
00:03:39,668 --> 00:03:41,418
With nothing to show for it.

68
00:03:41,728 --> 00:03:44,038
And your brain has a favorite phrase here.

69
00:03:44,428 --> 00:03:45,578
Just pay the minimum.

70
00:03:45,928 --> 00:03:47,198
It feels responsible.

71
00:03:47,648 --> 00:03:50,838
It's actually the most expensive habit on
this list.

72
00:03:51,208 --> 00:03:53,838
Now here's the one most people never
notice.

73
00:03:54,208 --> 00:03:56,058
It doesn't take money from you.

74
00:03:56,228 --> 00:03:58,658
It takes the money you were about to have.

75
00:03:59,359 --> 00:04:00,389
You get a raise!

76
00:04:00,599 --> 00:04:01,249
Congratulations!

77
00:04:01,799 --> 00:04:02,889
You earned it!

78
00:04:03,139 --> 00:04:08,389
Then, slowly, quietly, everything
upgrades.

79
00:04:08,939 --> 00:04:11,849
A slightly bigger place, a nicer car.

80
00:04:12,299 --> 00:04:14,949
More takeout because you're busy now.

81
00:04:15,339 --> 00:04:17,389
A year later, you earn more.

82
00:04:17,759 --> 00:04:20,489
But your savings look exactly the same.

83
00:04:20,779 --> 00:04:25,069
This is why so many people with good
salaries still feel broke.

84
00:04:25,419 --> 00:04:26,479
Your income went up.

85
00:04:26,499 --> 00:04:29,089
But so did your idea of normal.

86
00:04:29,559 --> 00:04:32,609
Your brain adjusts to every upgrade within
months.

87
00:04:32,719 --> 00:04:34,949
And then, it wants the next one.

88
00:04:35,339 --> 00:04:36,569
Here's a simple fix.

89
00:04:36,939 --> 00:04:41,929
The next time you get a raise, save at least half
of it before you get used to it.

90
00:04:42,019 --> 00:04:46,609
You'll still feel richer, and your future
self will actually be richer.

91
00:04:46,979 --> 00:04:50,179
And speaking of free money, this last one
is the

92
00:04:50,199 --> 00:04:53,529
most painful because you could fix it this
week.

93
00:04:53,839 --> 00:04:57,369
Many U.S. employers offer a 401(k) match.

94
00:04:57,739 --> 00:05:01,409
If you put in a few percent of your pay,
they add money on top.

95
00:05:01,719 --> 00:05:06,489
With a dollar-for-dollar match, every dollar
you put in instantly becomes two.

96
00:05:06,779 --> 00:05:10,109
No investment you'll ever find reliably
does that.

97
00:05:10,319 --> 00:05:14,509
And yet, plenty of people don't put in
enough to get the full match.

98
00:05:14,659 --> 00:05:19,349
They're turning down part of their own pay
while feeling guilty about a latte.

99
00:05:19,659 --> 00:05:21,419
Check your benefits page this week.

100
00:05:21,439 --> 00:05:24,369
That's the number I said might be sitting
on your phone.

101
00:05:25,118 --> 00:05:27,848
So does the coffee not matter at all?

102
00:05:28,278 --> 00:05:29,728
Here's the honest answer.

103
00:05:30,158 --> 00:05:34,348
The coffee isn't the problem, but it can
be a symptom.

104
00:05:34,838 --> 00:05:39,628
If you buy coffee on autopilot, you're probably
spending on autopilot everywhere.

105
00:05:40,338 --> 00:05:43,568
Subscriptions you forgot about, fees you
never check,

106
00:05:43,838 --> 00:05:46,208
apps that charge you every single month.

107
00:05:46,578 --> 00:05:49,588
So keep the coffee, just choose it on
purpose.

108
00:05:50,078 --> 00:05:54,888
Spend freely on what you love and cut hard
on what you don't even notice.

109
00:05:55,518 --> 00:05:59,768
Here are three moves for this week and none of
them involve giving up coffee.

110
00:06:00,338 --> 00:06:05,168
One, look at your three biggest bills,
housing, car, food.

111
00:06:05,558 --> 00:06:08,328
Find a 10% cut in just one of them.

112
00:06:08,658 --> 00:06:12,738
Two, list every debt with an interest rate
above 10%.

113
00:06:12,758 --> 00:06:15,288
That's your enemy, not your barista.

114
00:06:15,758 --> 00:06:19,898
Three, make sure you're getting your full
employer match and set your

115
00:06:19,918 --> 00:06:24,368
savings to go out automatically on payday
before your brain can spend it.

116
00:06:24,618 --> 00:06:25,988
So here's the truth.

117
00:06:26,338 --> 00:06:28,968
Your coffee was never what was keeping you
broke.

118
00:06:29,278 --> 00:06:32,928
The quiet stuff was, the car payment, the
interest,

119
00:06:33,238 --> 00:06:36,548
the raise you never saw, the free money
you never claimed.

120
00:06:36,918 --> 00:06:41,008
Fix those and you can enjoy every cup
without the guilt.

121
00:06:41,278 --> 00:06:44,578
And if you've ever wondered why, even when
you earn more,

122
00:06:44,598 --> 00:06:49,098
you still never feel rich, that's a whole
different trick your brain plays on you.

123
00:06:49,118 --> 00:06:50,708
Watch this one next.
